#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369265 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "756639e5-ef49-5356-874a-b797a567ade2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(5-2t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369267} {:answer/id #uuid "e3f350f1-37ad-503f-9ea6-70c30ae57437" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-t)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369268} {:answer/id #uuid "cb15b44a-28cb-5de2-b373-a77569fe3933" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3t+1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369269} {:answer/id #uuid "be76a975-6edc-58eb-8a4c-da7fbf2781b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+3t-1)\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369270} {:answer/id #uuid "68681886-c9c0-5332-b1f7-d6c36341760a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}(1-{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369271}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369268} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea741151-0b0f-527e-9bc4-e1287cde677a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0acc8f31-7302-5dee-af54-ae46e10b8d37" :question/math-academy-id "q-124266" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b21c9c11aa3320a729db0d8a71f5c89069d46527e78dfc76b47ae3925387c770.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(3x+2y)\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the line segment from the point $(-2,2)$ to the point $(-1,1),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨t-2,\\,2-t⟩.$*" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(-2,2)$ and $(-1,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(-2+t(-1-(-2))),\\,(2+t(1-2))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t-2,\\,2-t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=3x+2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =3(t-2)+2(2-t) \\\\  & =3t-6+4-2t \\\\  & =t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2)\\cdot (-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-t)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369272 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce947fb0-2af2-5578-bc88-f97fd8bc5195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369274} {:answer/id #uuid "7faeed0c-8c4d-51f9-816f-430b26355ead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369275} {:answer/id #uuid "d3b4e0df-ee12-54ca-90c1-55fd453aaffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369276} {:answer/id #uuid "17c11c14-5bd8-5dfc-9bc8-39959c08d204" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369277} {:answer/id #uuid "9230ca12-f5f1-5580-9077-a7bbc7def856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369278}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369275} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8f91420-1dd1-57a3-bbc3-7cd1a732535b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "bb41f199-2f33-5f68-9a53-dffe3c93ab84" :question/math-academy-id "q-124268" :question/problem "![](images/be/be3ad044a611d99e67c89d053d4878a2835be65828a4dc9300697bfd61822994.png)\n\nEvaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$ where $C$ is the path along the below-left quarter of the ellipse $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ traversed from the point $(-1,0)$ to the point $(0,-2),$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,\\pi \\le t\\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{\\int }_{\\pi }^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{u}^{3}\\,(-\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =-4{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{u}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{u}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =-[(0)^{4}-(-1)^{4}] \\\\  & =-(-1) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369873 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4dc0ac04-220f-5368-899c-f4b853d6caf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369875} {:answer/id #uuid "f515d3db-880a-5d23-99f0-9cb65e633ebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369876} {:answer/id #uuid "57254211-1cc3-5a7f-87e1-0c345d6ba0b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369877} {:answer/id #uuid "0ab4227a-a1c7-56de-9a4e-de04cc753871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369878} {:answer/id #uuid "47ca5fb3-37c5-5519-9b75-0484cfdf1808" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369879}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369875} :answer-field/id #uuid "c478e331-2d1a-585a-9599-198d7061ae49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "91c905cb-29ef-5cbc-a791-764e51ba7eed" :question/math-academy-id "q-160445" :question/problem "For the transformation $T,$ defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\ncompute the value of the Jacobian determinant at the point where $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{bmatrix}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have $x(u,v)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡u$ and $y(u,v)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡u)\\cdot (-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v)-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, when $\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\pi /4 \\\\ \\pi /4\\end{bmatrix},$ the value of our Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n{\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369999 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a085c46-201d-5ed3-be42-2030b699b7e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370001}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370001} :answer-field/id #uuid "2effdd59-bc2f-5ad0-a07a-23dc11d9d6e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b82726dc-a173-5a38-aefa-ebf1870acad4" :question/math-academy-id "q-277919" :question/problem "![](images/9b/9bb9fd502e37eb4e96c91c86a48833431b8f54c785f9dd096e8dc0842a2389f7.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=6$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7341349-5722-538a-a46b-c46b327387d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{225}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370004}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370004} :answer-field/id #uuid "24b13a75-2938-57e9-b343-79d1cfb33366" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d8eb6498-fe7d-54d8-8156-e7257b7cf3ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-279127" :question/problem "![](images/92/92752dfc375c895c72d3a053a9abfd64eb64ecf9660e24b0c731e7ae9616ae7b.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{15}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{15}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{225}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{225}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{25} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58f12915-dd40-5ab5-9c8d-d5b8239ddb65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370007} :answer-field/id #uuid "f45ec21e-afa6-5535-91ea-60e2824f8700" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ac9d8686-4f69-5072-a6be-b7169a3cf7dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-279141" :question/problem "![](images/24/244cfd754107b056748bd6049c2996f9c0a0ae990f2033005b2f96e5abf1c0ea.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=4$ and the vertical radius has length $b=9.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{9}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{81} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370008 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a34b4a4f-ec93-5b79-9836-24c30f1a306c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{225}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370010}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370010} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5e92adb-417b-51d5-9a32-861203d3639b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d4d8222d-c914-54e1-8111-88a395566578" :question/math-academy-id "q-279143" :question/problem "![](images/68/682eaeaa8b8ef428ab181953ab577e1fe91009996b83f0799a7684d8b9efb21d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=4$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{15}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{15}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{225}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{225} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370011 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e02416ae-0c05-5603-a201-57564dfca3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{289}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370013}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370013} :answer-field/id #uuid "a21d9fe8-5a2d-55ae-9ce6-47bd81a95ce0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1c883bff-1508-545d-b07f-f784ee6e3f19" :question/math-academy-id "q-279145" :question/problem "![](images/4e/4e5123d9581d8b9ee1cc32358519c505c47f6e976a2fa28ff1939c769bebcdc9.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{17}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{17}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{289}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{289} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370014 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "983ea1d4-591d-51d0-91a0-baacaa311a91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370016} {:answer/id #uuid "2dcb5f98-9095-45e6-b3f5-ea343bdb888d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4x^2}{25}+\\frac{4y^2}{49}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186720677}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370016} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc0c2ace-3d6d-5114-b445-c9cf46779b74" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "eb865dca-23c4-59c4-bd03-8c9d8005b669" :question/math-academy-id "q-279154" :question/problem "![](images/92/9291f02901a4a05150dfadd309cc7508209c0f249d9b07a8185d1259f67d7296.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{5}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{25}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{49}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d1f7bc7-0cdd-5b27-8fe9-02b806603eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{121}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370019}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370019} :answer-field/id #uuid "357e7c5e-4b53-50fa-852d-a2a0c73a078a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "11b50d3f-8a2a-53bd-9460-027070f70e56" :question/math-academy-id "q-279155" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9deebb67265aafd4fd2c3e7451dddc837c928242ac016a0632907816defcdec1.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{7}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{11}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{\\left(\\frac{11}{2}\\right)}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{49}{4}\\right)}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{\\left(\\frac{121}{4}\\right)} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{121} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0b4d7d8-6b13-51b3-8c16-f1f796b0790a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370022}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370022} :answer-field/id #uuid "1784709b-8c12-545a-96ba-dc167b20f7c0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370021} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e70a177c-e321-5d97-ad0f-2390d9f8f894" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370024}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370024} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c229291-398d-5b8b-8d76-841b2f6ef86c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2a5d8fed-b754-51cf-933f-f6f47cc1ecb4" :question/math-academy-id "q-279198" :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=8$ and ${b}^{2}=24.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=8,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=24,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{24}=2\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{6},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{6}=4\\sqrt{6},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370993 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65eb186c-e14b-5e5b-b246-910f532877ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370995} {:answer/id #uuid "ba12a02d-2a23-5848-a775-1a9517753135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370996} {:answer/id #uuid "0d8fdc06-cbcf-5c84-9ddf-a56eb7032043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370997} {:answer/id #uuid "ceeee171-e500-5146-8d03-5a5c1bd03a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370998} {:answer/id #uuid "4041e09f-b99a-5bc3-af3a-1a97f34bb588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370999}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370998} :answer-field/id #uuid "47ed664f-5269-578c-9dc2-0e5ea600cf72" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0bf44446-fd59-5eea-85f3-78a8c0b563be" :question/math-academy-id "q-69890" :question/problem "![](images/2a/2a7f7fc248f5582afea5240aa2f7c5f6ee421a53961640efbfae18b9c920c10d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=4$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371665 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6291ddda-70de-5718-9bfc-105bebc04e63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371667} {:answer/id #uuid "9c909eaf-192d-5156-bfd4-507f1094667b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=i+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371668} {:answer/id #uuid "c96f1d9d-d0ba-5ae3-bcdf-556a56778e37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=-2\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371669} {:answer/id #uuid "cef6452c-f105-5828-9426-ff34265362da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=3\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371670} {:answer/id #uuid "15bc72b2-4db5-5aa4-9cbf-0714e10fa1a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371671}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371670} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9373a90-9549-57f3-9c97-5391475a1938" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f6439da6-7b28-559c-9b9e-cf7883c3a650" :question/math-academy-id "q-122662" :question/problem "![](images/8d/8d978a63c661ddb2adfca6f0be5315778abb2464a1e1f45f78dedfa4cbaa5d12.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $y$-axis. So, the $i$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nH(x,y,z)=i+k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *positive* $y$-direction, so the $j$ component of our vector field is positive. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=-2\\,j\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $j$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $G(x,y,z)=3\\,j.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371749 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "421dd9cb-d37a-51b2-bf54-7fecaeaa111e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+e\\,j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371751} {:answer/id #uuid "40e107dd-903b-532c-a936-fd8a0507a6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e\\,i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371752} {:answer/id #uuid "62840a4a-671f-55d2-ae1f-8ee90cadeddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+j+e\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371753} {:answer/id #uuid "935eb2f0-2841-5ad9-bc69-e37faff9662c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "i+e\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371754} {:answer/id #uuid "6a087499-3ac4-5430-b783-46e925a0bb89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\,i+j+k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371755}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371751} :answer-field/id #uuid "30f4d423-1557-5b46-a95f-3cf4f20875a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371750}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4e0286fb-4c1d-5cce-a9a8-0a29399f515e" :question/math-academy-id "q-131119" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{y}+y{e}^{z}+z{e}^{x},$ find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(0,0,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(0,0,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+y{e}^{z}+z{e}^{x})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+y{e}^{z}+z{e}^{x})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{e}^{y}+y{e}^{z}+z{e}^{x})\\,k \\\\  & =({e}^{y}+z{e}^{x})\\,i+(x{e}^{y}+{e}^{z})\\,j+(y{e}^{z}+{e}^{x})\\,k \\\\ ∇f(0,0,1) & =({e}^{0}+(1){e}^{0})i+((0){e}^{0}+{e}^{1})j+((0){e}^{1}+{e}^{0})k \\\\  & =2\\,i+e\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $2\\,i+e\\,j+k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(0,0,1).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db65b2cf-8271-5607-a640-ae769caddf03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371874} {:answer/id #uuid "23bd3ecb-441d-5a27-8b9f-49934c6dbae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371875} {:answer/id #uuid "934e91be-6058-5dc5-ba33-86ee65e0a431" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371876} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8bd3b1-0508-595d-9241-fa8262df8825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371877} {:answer/id #uuid "57283ea2-bea1-548c-804f-95b9c1485077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371878}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371876} :answer-field/id #uuid "6938f289-182f-5501-8657-403464e01c47" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ff7a088d-dba6-5fcf-8b51-57e65b4c9e89" :question/math-academy-id "q-160654" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y=\\frac{3}{x}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}y=2,\\,{x}^{2}y=4,\\,xy=1,\\,xy=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}y,\\,v=xy\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u={x}^{2}y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{2}y=2\\,⟹\\,u=2 \\\\  & {x}^{2}y=4\\,⟹\\,u=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=xy$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & xy=3\\,⟹\\,v=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/ba/baa8e8ab6cff8edec84d3f0d0950931f247153523e3320abbdf673161afb44ae.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2xy & {x}^{2} \\\\ y & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}y-{x}^{2}y \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2dc75e0-ec1a-5f7b-8a43-e987a45ded5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371881} {:answer/id #uuid "31e24d23-144c-5f4c-83da-8b52dfbbc484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371882} {:answer/id #uuid "3b49b980-f639-5d69-be29-e580d2912e63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371883} {:answer/id #uuid "055627e2-cfed-53bd-bcf2-ede52aba6dfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371884} {:answer/id #uuid "55b60d42-b61b-57d5-8be6-96dc868fced6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371885}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371882} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb25176c-9969-5abb-b583-813ca366126d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cbbbf3ae-4ecb-5df7-bf03-c3a7be26b224" :question/math-academy-id "q-160852" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\ny=3x-1,\\,y=3x-3,\\,x=3y,\\,x=3y+2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n3x-y=1,\\,3x-y=3,\\,x-3y=0,\\,x-3y=2\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=3x-y,\\,v=x-3y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=3x-y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 3x-y=1\\,⟹\\,u=1 \\\\  & 3x-y=3\\,⟹\\,u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=x-3y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & x-3y=0\\,⟹\\,v=0 \\\\  & x-3y=2\\,⟹\\,v=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/a8/a873a6bdd07f942d9c7e729e55354b6414259a2f38b52e7faf1ad0befa2c827c.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-9+1 \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{8}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot (3-1)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371886 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ccecda64-e614-576a-95ef-e05124675fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371888} {:answer/id #uuid "c3dbc0ee-bd56-5d09-82ae-0a50d663a617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371889} {:answer/id #uuid "7e1c272c-a26e-56c4-b420-6c0e9237be97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371890} {:answer/id #uuid "ad9488a7-26bc-5807-abd0-f5550f336827" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371891} {:answer/id #uuid "49a9bf82-6b9b-5af5-bd83-8f2fc3e179b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371892}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371890} :answer-field/id #uuid "f732fda0-f223-5e8e-9cd7-3bad366f7805" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371887}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1533ae23-2a8c-5746-8cc6-278953144598" :question/math-academy-id "q-160857" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the following lines.\n\n$$\n3y=4x+1,\\,3y=4x-2,\\,3x=2y+1,\\,3x=2y+3\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\n4x-3y=-1,\\,4x-3y=2,\\,3x-2y=1,\\,3x-2y=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=4x-3y,\\,v=3x-2y\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=4x-3y$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 4x-3y=-1\\,⟹\\,u=-1 \\\\  & 4x-3y=2\\,⟹\\,u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=3x-2y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 3x-2y=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & 3x-2y=3\\,⟹\\,v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/5d/5dc4be989300dca3e52b7ff1dfb6a21c60409976fe55d42f5ae530f033725a64.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-8+9 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(2-(-1))\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "184368e1-d4ec-53a7-b724-26ecbd05d7dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371901} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd23822-1245-54b3-b063-1a3e5b75308f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371902} {:answer/id #uuid "04872f3c-0156-57be-a135-c6b36ffb10ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371903} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd73d74-b8b1-5322-8406-42b2b8fdfb02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371904} {:answer/id #uuid "8428d403-ae08-501f-9369-f40eb9366d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371905}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371905} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba19557e-03f5-528c-ad20-5f4ba059cdd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b5d19e11-2889-5a4f-a620-f1b6f728a9db" :question/math-academy-id "q-160870" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y-3x,\\,v=y+2x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the lines\n\n$$\ny=3x-2,\\,y=3x+2,\\,y=1-2x,\\,y=3-2x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-3x,\\,v=y+2x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=y-3x$ into the equations of the first and second lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=3x-2 & ⟹\\, & y-3x=-2 & ⟹\\, & u=-2 \\\\  & y=3x+2 & ⟹\\, & y-3x=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=y+2x$ into the equations of the third and fourth lines:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=1-2x & ⟹\\, & y+2x=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=3-2x & ⟹\\, & y+2x=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/9c/9c17c81b4abc0d4ebd1eadfe951b0eb0c2674d9eb07b785e7fd972fd00e513ae.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{5}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\text{Area}(Δ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371906 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33a307a4-5e3d-5e26-9870-e4654bb643c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371908} {:answer/id #uuid "9175a99b-56d0-595b-86db-511244542711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371909} {:answer/id #uuid "6ae05fa3-0d0d-5167-94c2-8ea479a334e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371910} {:answer/id #uuid "222f9a03-a010-5d70-a31b-68c15b8ab921" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371911} {:answer/id #uuid "86cdb592-c0ee-5680-989f-fa4c9b6fb697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371912}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371909} :answer-field/id #uuid "579f73d3-5ee3-5e3b-9cb0-45b6635a75cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f411b679-c6ae-56bb-9f50-ac0896593964" :question/math-academy-id "q-160885" :question/problem "Using the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=y+2x,\\,v=y-2x,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ bounded by the lines $y+2x=1,$ $y-2x=1$ and the curve ${y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}=4.$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=y+2x,\\,v=y-2x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $y+2x=u$ into the equation of the first line, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2x=1\\,⟹\\,u=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile substituting $y-2x=v$ into the equation of the second line, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2x=1\\,⟹\\,v=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $u=y+2x$ and $v=y-2x$ into the equation of curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4{x}^{2}=4\\,⟹\\,(y+2x)(y-2x)=4,\\,⟹\\,uv=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe intersection point of the two lines in the $uv$-plane is $(1,1).$\n\nTo find the intersection of the curve with the first line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}uv &=4 \\\\ u &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=1 \\\\ v &=4.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the intersections of the curve with the second line in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}uv &=4 \\\\ v &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}u &=4 \\\\ v &=1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the intersection points are $(1,1),$ $(1,4)$ and $(4,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le \\frac{4}{u}\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/30/30fff9564451baadff3cae48dbfb91dc08fc8591e596e7cd2f65d8cfc6c9c5a6.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(4)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{1}{4}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[{\\int }_{1}^{4/u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}[v]_{1}^{4/u}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}(\\frac{4}{u}-1)du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[4\\operatorname{ln}⁡u-u]}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-4+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d59625-eac9-589e-ab14-341551eea08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371915} {:answer/id #uuid "7b889d37-5d45-5818-9d27-40379a4533ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371916} {:answer/id #uuid "b06248ff-2151-5554-b5af-ab842ba5705a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371917} {:answer/id #uuid "a1e1a6f5-fc15-5138-88ce-fdee68bb88d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371918} {:answer/id #uuid "5b52d6ac-ffd9-5f34-ba9e-30bc4a86d030" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371919}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371916} :answer-field/id #uuid "49ac9b17-c604-511f-9b48-3b87989affec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "75940fb8-f294-5aab-8f7b-cfe4e75211a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-160891" :question/problem "Using the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{xy},\\,v={x}^{2}y,\n$$\n\ncalculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the curves\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2x},\\,y=\\frac{1}{3x},\\,y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\,y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{1}{xy},\\,v={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=\\frac{1}{xy}$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{2x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{xy}=2 & ⟹\\, & u=2 \\\\  & y=\\frac{1}{3x} & ⟹\\, & \\frac{1}{xy}=3 & ⟹\\, & u=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v={x}^{2}y$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & ⟹\\, & {x}^{2}y=1 & ⟹\\, & v=1 \\\\  & y=\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}} & ⟹\\, & {x}^{2}y=3 & ⟹\\, & v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 3,\\,1\\le v\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\n![](images/05/05d2ca47db245b147cb52f95a474a60ece144931c8b6e1e40c6628a99b53d690.png)\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}y} & -\\frac{1}{x{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2xy & \\text{ }\\text{ }{x}^{2}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=y=\\frac{{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}y}=\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}v}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371974 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "401d1340-9f46-59c1-abfc-5071d62dc570" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371976} {:answer/id #uuid "3204bc80-8ab3-5312-ac12-252b1d788312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371977} {:answer/id #uuid "545f9367-80fc-527f-b725-9577794d6914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371978} {:answer/id #uuid "6d8e3cd4-7080-5e0f-b3c0-62f9a1c245aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371979} {:answer/id #uuid "2897d53d-b8f5-5abb-a72a-895d82c614bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371980} :answer-field/id #uuid "f77b7f7b-0f4d-55ec-986c-8391703e056e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f2acf3d8-4862-5fab-a9f5-360ef711c3dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-161869" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to calculate the area of the finite region $D$ in the first quadrant bounded by the following curves and lines.\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{x},\\,y=\\frac{4}{x},\\,x={y}^{2},\\,x=3{y}^{2}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows: \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWe'll assume this transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv,\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in five steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nNotice that we can rewrite the equations of our curves as follows:\n\n$$\nxy=1,\\,xy=4,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=1,\\,\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=3\n$$\n\nThis suggests the following change of variables:\n\n$$\nu=xy,\\,v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nWe substitute $u=xy$ into the equations of the first and second curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & xy=1\\,⟹\\,u=1 \\\\  & xy=4\\,⟹\\,u=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd we substitute $v=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ into the equations of the third and fourth curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,v=1 \\\\  & \\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}=3\\,⟹\\,v=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane is the following rectangle:\n\n$$\nΔ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,1\\le v\\le 3}\n$$\n\n![](images/2d/2d98eb2de472226d010fb633478f3662754679c2256e3fd3d06600a6599bc7f5.png)\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}y & x \\\\ \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}} & -\\frac{2x}{{y}^{3}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2x}{{y}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3x}{{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =-3v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{3v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\text{d}u{\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{1}{v}\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot (\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3886a830-f807-532a-8dff-ac10e7ec4914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-72\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372022} {:answer/id #uuid "7607b49a-d23b-55b8-8e3c-b7353e88eeeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "144\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372023} {:answer/id #uuid "c46e2782-3e03-5ada-93b7-ba596554275e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-36\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372024} {:answer/id #uuid "0f26e94b-0e7f-527e-a515-9f633c3e5b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372025} {:answer/id #uuid "5c50dc6b-1088-5cb6-baa6-a6a933d4f39e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372026}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372022} :answer-field/id #uuid "10810889-3a2d-5477-b575-bc6677b334ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "da6c973e-9e1d-506f-8988-7e41ce9a8511" :question/math-academy-id "q-162371" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨y,\\,2yz,\\,-{z}^{2}+1⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the half-cone $z=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=-3.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the half-cone $z=-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and the plane $z=-3.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=-3.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-3)=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨y,\\,2yz,\\,-{z}^{2}+1⟩{|}_{z=-3} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{-6y}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{-8}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot 0-(-6y)\\cdot 0-8)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-8\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9$ with radius $3$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-8\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}-8\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-8{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-8[\\theta ]_{\\theta =0}^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}]}_{r=0}^{r=3} \\\\  & =-8(2\\pi -0)\\cdot (\\frac{9}{2}-0) \\\\  & =-72\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372027 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff3a9c27-f34b-5594-94af-40a7c58297f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372029} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a89c10-18a7-59cb-8a1b-3a18b2a90938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372030} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ff01ff-40d6-5616-bd3a-9376f9415536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372031} {:answer/id #uuid "14cd568a-621b-5de0-baca-80df932e47d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372032} {:answer/id #uuid "daa9659f-982a-5882-a68f-e91cd62724ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372033}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372032} :answer-field/id #uuid "5674f149-9ce3-5602-89ab-1339d40392d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "00a16c1c-6465-5edc-becb-61f3932da901" :question/math-academy-id "q-162372" :question/problem "Use Stokes' theorem to evaluate \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨-y,\\,z,\\,-2x⟩,$ and $C$ is the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=2+2x-y.$ The curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above." :question/worked-solution "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by a simple, closed, piecewise-smooth boundary $C$ with positive orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $S,$ then Stokes' theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nThere are many surfaces whose boundary is the curve $C$ representing the intersection of the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ and the plane $z=2+2x-y.$ One such surface would be the plane $S$ with the equation \n\n$$\nz=g(x,y)=2+2x-y.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2+2x-y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+2x-y)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into the expression for $\\text{curl}\\,F:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F\\,{|}_{z=g(x,y)} & =⟨-y,\\,z,\\,-2x⟩{|}_{z=2+2x-y} \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{-y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{2+2x-y}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{-2x}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(-y)\\cdot (2)-(2+2x-y)\\cdot (-1)-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y+2+2x-y-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2+y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is region enclosed by the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane.\n\nNotice that the projection of $C$ onto the $xy$-plane is the circle ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1$ with radius $1$ centered at the origin. Therefore, using the change of variables to polar coordinates\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta ,\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2+y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}2r+{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[{r}^{2}+\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(1+\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[\\theta -\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372034 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6596a7e1-33c8-5770-ad3d-4bd6746339f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=(6{x}^{2}+3xy)\\,i-3{x}^{2}y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372036} {:answer/id #uuid "1019de8d-9796-54d8-a7d7-3867ddbc355e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=-3{x}^{2}\\,i-(3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372037} {:answer/id #uuid "db8a7224-0ec3-5591-a0e7-169572aceb42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=(3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,i+6xy\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372038} {:answer/id #uuid "98c5d095-62c9-5b47-a91b-d7f4bc4717c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=(3{x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2})\\,i-3{x}^{2}y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372039} {:answer/id #uuid "af0a43c0-1af9-50b5-8508-84ab433607d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=-6xy\\,i-(3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372040}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372040} :answer-field/id #uuid "c25bb45f-532f-5ba4-9fd3-2f3c979bc106" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "65d2f94e-229a-51fd-a26e-3f0815f0eb18" :question/math-academy-id "q-162492" :question/problem "Construct the vector field that has the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-6xy, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}+3{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)={x}^{3}-3x{y}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j \\\\  & =-6xy\\,i-(3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4c35f35-505d-5b86-9167-ceefb238af14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\,i+({x}^{2}-x)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372043} {:answer/id #uuid "0b599044-693d-54d2-b820-f738cb7bb060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=(-2x+1)\\,i+2y\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372044} {:answer/id #uuid "e4b4cd56-a295-5ee8-bdb9-0604ff265037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=(2y+x)\\,i+2x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372045} {:answer/id #uuid "2e5b38c9-5492-5744-91b2-bc509b4b6280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=(y+1)\\,i-{x}^{2}\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372046} {:answer/id #uuid "1dea751c-2b96-5f6e-8cf0-07f9421b0d5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=2y\\,i+(2x-1)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372047}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372047} :answer-field/id #uuid "57871c32-9592-5d5b-b52f-978468b21542" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fea8348f-c35f-5297-af8d-bffb135e2427" :question/math-academy-id "q-162493" :question/problem "Construct the vector field that has the stream function $ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+x.$" :question/worked-solution "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =2y, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+x) \\\\  & =2x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}+x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j \\\\  & =2y\\,i+(2x-1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9de0e353-ab6e-59bc-a0ed-36c03e15387b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372050} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8be957-5e7a-5c11-9e63-50310cedd0c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372051} {:answer/id #uuid "42f0507b-1475-5ce6-b8db-506235509208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372052} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa5c9a6-5913-5d25-b0d5-b24f5f1a2717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372053} {:answer/id #uuid "7729c754-88f0-530c-869b-5bdc5168a17b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i+({e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-1)\\,j" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372054}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372051} :answer-field/id #uuid "b231bed0-a79c-51f5-8734-2ba409222c93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "43559bcb-c65a-577f-8d0a-2160e10d1cc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-162494" :question/problem "Construct the vector field that has the stream function $ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x.$" :question/worked-solution "A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if the following equations hold:\n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}\n$$\n\nComputing the partial derivatives of the function $ψ(x,y),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, \\\\ Q & =-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x) \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector field with the stream function $ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y) & =P\\,i+Q\\,j \\\\  & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aeb916fe-deac-5c6b-a3af-a1e29579a5af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372064} {:answer/id #uuid "ee658281-8425-5be1-8788-54102c3c6175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372065} {:answer/id #uuid "660c3c54-a204-504f-ae3d-31a70939bfb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372066} {:answer/id #uuid "160f71b3-9073-5c2f-99f9-acde1bf550a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372067} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0c4912-d2bd-5e3f-9221-2060507e5033" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372068}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372067} :answer-field/id #uuid "337543b8-07ff-5870-85da-be792a9109dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9fb4a1a9-80b2-5c9b-b44c-5418f559f461" :question/math-academy-id "q-162518" :question/problem "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+(-{e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=g=0,$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)={e}^{x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bdb66e66-97ce-510e-91ff-36e680214001" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=2x{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372071} {:answer/id #uuid "98d6bc39-1444-5191-a3ad-5fe791958a80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372072} {:answer/id #uuid "337e4203-10f9-512a-9b23-65ec7a8da398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)={x}^{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372073} {:answer/id #uuid "0999957a-e07e-5505-ae6f-43a0cb1f8759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-x{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372074} {:answer/id #uuid "e09b23ec-9538-5a09-8cef-d2471ca0ea5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372075}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372072} :answer-field/id #uuid "0298ba8f-3875-5cfc-99ba-d4ad417d980d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372070}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "91d3698a-850c-538b-a0de-a75020df1c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-162519" :question/problem "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)=3({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,i-6xy\\,j?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=3({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,i-6xy\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=3({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})$ and $Q(x,y)=-6xy.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=6x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=6x+(-6x)=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we find a stream function. A function $ψ(x,y)$ is a stream function of the vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ if \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y},\\,Q=-\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂ψ}{∂y}=3({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂ψ}{∂x}=6xy.\n$$\n\nIntegrating these equations, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}+f(x),\\,ψ(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y+g(y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ and $g$ are arbitrary functions. Finally, setting $f=0,$ and $g(y)=-{y}^{3},$ we have\n\n$$\nψ(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67b8f6d6-9607-5514-abbd-603cb08ece42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$F$ has no stream function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372078} {:answer/id #uuid "72008387-8f3a-5d1d-965c-282a4ef1a635" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372079} {:answer/id #uuid "7b24f282-d62b-5e80-a78c-10312ff8606e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372080} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7e266d-e3fe-59e3-b2ef-457c0782331f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372081} {:answer/id #uuid "be1595e1-671d-5e1a-95b7-d83ff63e6f83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)=-xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372082}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372078} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cada9ed-c85a-58fb-a254-1769ba4f3d5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "622f220e-fb66-51d7-b3ac-edc3a1b8924a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162520" :question/problem "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,i-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)\\,i-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)$ and $Q(x,y)=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a no stream function, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=(1-{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy)-xy\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\ne 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e32f2c3b-fa9c-5bf6-a35b-57839d0e21d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$F$ has no stream function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372085} {:answer/id #uuid "703dbab9-9c69-5646-a97b-dd8ef0a32c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}+2xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372086} {:answer/id #uuid "b03ae81f-442d-5398-a1d0-eb48182ed670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372087} {:answer/id #uuid "06d4c7ba-315f-5ed3-b710-82a77b517287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)={y}^{2}+xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372088} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb65d35-dc25-51cf-95b6-281265b1d418" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "ψ(x,y)={x}^{2}-4y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372089}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372085} :answer-field/id #uuid "126114a5-a04b-5b6e-a5e7-1561c7926bd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186372084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5fa02a5c-65fb-5f22-a3ed-8e6c445f1c62" :question/math-academy-id "q-162522" :question/problem "Which of the following is a stream function for the vector field\n$F(x,y)=2xy\\,i+({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,j?$" :question/worked-solution "A vector field $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ with a simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2}$ has a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ if and only if\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.\n$$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=2xy\\,i+({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=2xy$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}-{x}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $F$ has a no stream function, since\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=2y+2y=4y\\ne 0.\n$$"}]}
